  "author_source_solution_checks": [
    {
      "source_number": "682",
      "question_ids": [
        "q682-order"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "263410f72a4f560ac2eefc0e1ff171049d13dfa847a7991f56da5f823a4120e0",
      "author_solution": "682. Task: compare three exponential pairs. Since 6/7 < 7/8,\nsqrt(3)<sqrt(7), and -sqrt(5)<-sqrt(3), all three first expressions\nare smaller than their second expressions. Increasing base 2 preserves\nthe order even for negative irrational exponents.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 682"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q682-order/A: 48/56 < 49/56 and base 2 is increasing. q682-order/C: -sqrt(5) < -sqrt(3); increasing base preserves this order. q682-order/D: sqrt(3) < sqrt(7); increasing base preserves this order. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All three original comparisons appear as complete relational claims, with a second alternative for the radical comparison and a false equality for the fractional comparison. Reversing B/D is a normal competing claim, not redundant duplicate wording."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "683",
      "question_ids": [
        "q683-constants",
        "q683-variable"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "5d356a2cf0f1a6bff5c702b02cd7fd99b5039921320fbd48f0a2cddc6e5f4146",
      "author_solution": "683. Task: compare eight expressions with 1, including variable cases.\na<1, b>1, c<1, d>1, e>1 (2-sqrt(2)>0), f<1 (1-sqrt(3)<0).\nFor g, 2^x is below/equal/above 1 as x<0 / x=0 / x>0.\nFor h, 2^(1-x) is below/equal/above 1 as x>1 / x=1 / x<1.\nDo not fix x to obtain a single comparison.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 683"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q683-constants/B: Exponent signs are negative, positive, negative, positive, positive, negative; base 2 puts the powers on corresponding sides of 1. q683-variable/C: Monotonicity and exponent zeros give thresholds 0 and 1 with the displayed opposite sign sequences. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (2 questions). All six constant exponents retain their signs, radicals and tiny positive exponent. Complete-column recognition retains comparison with 1, including the near-zero distractor. Both real-variable cases preserve all three sign regions and equality boundaries; the shifted/reflected exponent is not treated as x."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "684",
      "question_ids": [
        "q684-images"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "66d223ef7ae4f668592f1e637dbf668c6f01fa047c43900f59106715327e5a87",
      "author_solution": "684. Task: image sets with endpoint topology.\na (1,infinity); b (0,1); c (2,32); d [1/2,2]; e [1,2].\nStrict monotonicity transports finite endpoints, retains their open/closed\nstatus, and an unattained horizontal asymptote does not become an endpoint.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 684"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q684-images/A: Increasing positive 2^x maps the two open half-lines to (1,infinity) and (0,1). q684-images/B: The open endpoints 1,5 map to 2,32 without inclusion. q684-images/D: Included endpoints give [1/2,2] and [1,2]. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All five source intervals are present. Open/closed finite boundaries and nonattainment of the zero asymptote are tested, not only endpoint numbers."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "685",
      "question_ids": [
        "q685-inverse"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "e69bef381eac9b254e2ec4cb3ebabf8f80de873d25ece447e7461d3058fba377",
      "author_solution": "685. Task: six inverse exponential equations. RHS are 2^5,2^-6,2^-1,\n2^-2,2^-3,2^7; uniqueness gives x=5,-6,-1,-2,-3,7 respectively.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 685"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q685-inverse/C: 32,1/64,0.5,0.25,0.125,128 are respectively powers with exponents 5,-6,-1,-2,-3,7. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All six equations remain in their original order. Vector recognition loses independent written inverse derivations but does not delete an equation or change a right-hand side."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "686",
      "question_ids": [
        "q686-bounds"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "3500cfadfcc4c2c5aa5e9c4974e59d8bab5417a5f3e6347ac21d5858a7919a84",
      "author_solution": "686. Task: four solution sets.\na [8,infinity); b (-infinity,-2]; c (-7,infinity); d (-infinity,1).\nRewrite 256=2^8, .25=2^-2, 1/128=2^-7, 2=2^1.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 686"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q686-bounds/A: 256=2^8; increasing comparison gives x>=8. q686-bounds/B: 0.25=2^-2; increasing comparison gives x<=-2. q686-bounds/D: 1/128=2^-7 gives x>-7. q686-bounds/E: 2=2^1 gives x<1. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All four source inequalities retain strictness and the two non-strict boundaries. C/D and E/F discriminate direction and endpoint inclusion."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "687",
      "question_ids": [
        "q687-changes"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "dc261bed81cbe212509154eb3d3db6dcbbc4ae344b0172103284ac3edd9fa9ba",
      "author_solution": "687. Task: compare two pairs of vertical changes from the graph.\nFor width h>0, delta at starting x=t is 2^t(2^h-1).\na later interval [1.5,2.5] has twice the change of [.5,1.5].\nb later interval [1.2,1.7] has twice the change of [.2,.7].\nBoth are vertical changes, not final heights or slopes at isolated points.\nAn actual graph must remain available; algebra alone loses the requested route.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 687"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q687-changes/B: For both pairs the one-unit translate doubles the positive vertical difference, matching the steeper right-hand graph. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). Both original interval pairs are shown in the stem and lie inside graph A. The learner compares vertical increments, not merely the equal horizontal widths. No drawn increment or supplied factor answers the task."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "688",
      "question_ids": [
        "q688-crossing"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "d41cce92a2c67b64a03163d28c8703d103b274676e1599a3391da737193243e8",
      "author_solution": "688. Task: sketch both curves together, solve equality and both inequalities\ngraphically. Positive increasing curves meet only at (0,1).\n1.5^x/2^x=(.75)^x, so equality x=0, first smaller for x>0,\nfirst larger for x<0. Curve order reverses across the common point.\nProvide actual visible graphs; recognition cannot establish drawing ability.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 688"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q688-crossing/A: Both powers equal 1 at x=0 and their ratio is (4/3)^x, equaling 1 only there. q688-crossing/C: The base ratio exceeds 1, giving first<second exactly for x>0. q688-crossing/D: The first power is larger exactly for x<0. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). The two specified functions share one actual graph. Equality and both strict inequalities are retained. Free graph construction becomes interpretation of a supplied graph, a concrete production loss."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "689",
      "question_ids": [
        "q689-partition"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "32aabdad08b5e336e0a1112cbb2a17370f4a93f4347f9460b255c52e66547c7d",
      "author_solution": "689. Task: two triples of equality/greater/less solution sets.\nBoth ratios 15/8 and .9/.8 are >1. In both triples equality {0},\ngreater (0,infinity), less (-infinity,0). Comparing bases rather than\nchecking the exponent's sign gives the wrong conclusion for negative x.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 689"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q689-partition/C: Both ratios exceed 1 and give equality at zero, greater on positive x, less on negative x. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All six equality/greater/less source tasks are retained in one full partition for each base pair; decreasing individual functions do not reverse comparison between bases at fixed x."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "690",
      "question_ids": [
        "q690-source-graph"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "141277effeadb33d6ee3e6055c9cd12900a05c04a7f9a3e402c4148ffb12668d",
      "author_solution": "690. Task: two mutual-position comparisons of the actual three curves.\nFor x>0: .25^x < (1/3)^x < .8^x.\nFor x<0: .25^x > (1/3)^x > .8^x. At zero all equal 1.\nUse source/modern Figure 76, never substitute a symbolic-only prompt.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 690"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q690-source-graph/A: For x>0 the base order .25<1/3<.8 is preserved, matching the right side. q690-source-graph/B: For x<0 reciprocal powers reverse the base order, matching the left side. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). The current figure-76 SVG exactly matches the seed binding. The authorized source PNG and styled full current view were interpreted once; geometry PASS is reused. Both requested half-planes use the three specifically named curves."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "691",
      "question_ids": [
        "q691-family"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "6c52d1d96a36a3ed4849c2bda17274eafd2c4448ec30ac01742737ac8b9be366",
      "author_solution": "691. Task: sketch two arbitrary curves for 0<a<b<1.\nBoth decrease, are positive, pass through (0,1), approach zero on the right,\nand grow without bound on the left. a^x<b^x for x>0 and a^x>b^x for x<0.\nTheir precise steepness is not fixed by the source; any numerical example\nmust be identified as a representative, not silently set a and b.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 691"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q691-family/A: Both bases are below 1, so both curves decrease and a^0=b^0=1. q691-family/B: Positive powers preserve base order; negative powers reverse it. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). The universal condition 0<a<b<1 remains explicit; the figure bases are explicitly examples, not replacements for general a,b. Sketch production becomes general-property recognition using both sides of an actual shared graph."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "692",
      "question_ids": [
        "q692-middle"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "d3a941c8069239b9368ed58640edb36396dc474e37ee9c5b361bf3b9c3bfd175",
      "author_solution": "692. Task: sketch bases 1/2,1/3 and place .4 between them.\nSince 1/3<.4<1/2, for x>0: (1/3)^x<.4^x<(1/2)^x;\nfor x<0 the order reverses. All pass through (0,1) and decrease.\nThe middle graph is not an arithmetic average of heights.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 692"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q692-middle/C: The intermediate base gives the intermediate curve on both sides, with reversed exterior order; every zero power is 1. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All three original formulas are shown and both sides of zero are judged. The common point is a helpful added relation, not a lost source subtask; all alternatives sharing it means that extra relation is not independently discriminated."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "693",
      "question_ids": [
        "q693-constant-crossing"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "963e1376626f8d2e8d8ba3fc08d8c4d60e952bbef0a9ff0776240990bc98adcc",
      "author_solution": "693. Task: for both base regimes, sketch a^x and the constant a, compare.\nThey meet at (1,a) only. If a>1, a^x<a for x<1 and >a for x>1.\nIf 0<a<1, a^x>a for x<1 and <a for x>1.\nThe threshold is 1, not 0, despite the common exponential reference (0,1).",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 693"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q693-constant-crossing/A: a^x=a iff x=1; increasing and decreasing regimes give opposite inequalities on either side. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). Both base regimes remain universal and the representative bases are disclosed. The constant is y=a, not y=1; all before/at/after intersection positions are retained. Supplied graphs replace drawing."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "694",
      "question_ids": [
        "q694-reciprocal"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "9485bb76cd4345b65a03d926116d4b6995ccac2b60d055b2f293f03c774ed11b",
      "author_solution": "694. Task: three comparisons using reciprocals.\na .04^-5=25^5 <25^6; b (2/3)^-6=(3/2)^6=1.5^6;\nc .5^-11=2^11. A negative exponent reciprocates the base, not the value's sign.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 694"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q694-reciprocal/A: .04^-5=25^5<25^6 since 25>1. q694-reciprocal/B: (2/3)^-6=(3/2)^6=1.5^6. q694-reciprocal/D: .5^-11=2^11. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All three original comparisons retained. Equivalent reciprocal bases, equality and positive range are distinguished; no answer-bearing prose precedes the claims."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "695",
      "question_ids": [
        "q695-monotonicity"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "9d125c3f42d48105cd1b12598261a7ed0d9c7217782c5469bd89af78e74187f6",
      "author_solution": "695. Task: infer k,m order for four bases.\na k>m because pi>1.\nb k<m because pi/3>1 (pi>3), despite the base being close to 1.\nc k<m because 0<sqrt(3)-sqrt(2)<1 and order reverses.\nd k<m because sqrt(7)-1>1.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 695"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q695-monotonicity/B: pi and pi/3 exceed 1, sqrt(3)-sqrt(2) is in (0,1), and sqrt(7)-1 exceeds 1; the sequence is >,<,<,<. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All four independent hypotheses remain separate; the stem explicitly prevents incorrectly treating the four k,m pairs as one inconsistent system. Base classification still has to be judged."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "696",
      "question_ids": [
        "q696-one"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "6ff8c2ffd3e0de2ecbde1875ee141bfe39f82fb80f2c020173287018995d0f78",
      "author_solution": "696. Task: four comparisons to 1. a<1, b<1, c>1, d>1.\nFor c sqrt(15)/4<1 and exponent negative; for d sqrt(3)-sqrt(2)\nis between 0 and 1 and exponent negative. Do not treat irrational bases as >1.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 696"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q696-one/C: The first two are below 1; the two subunit bases raised to negative exponents exceed 1. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All four source expressions, signs and fractional exponents remain. Comparing bases with 1 and accounting for negative powers is still required."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "697",
      "question_ids": [
        "q697-signs"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "704b24f6d4bf9fd52e6a3873d5d7b9a2620171da4805c3c113b576ce78b22734",
      "author_solution": "697. Task: infer the signs of four exponents.\na positive, b negative, c positive, d negative. Compare RHS with 1;\nthen use base monotonicity. The magnitude 1.003 being near 1 does not make k zero.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 697"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q697-signs/A: For bases 5 and 7 above 1, the right sides exceeding 1 imply positive exponents. q697-signs/B: For bases .3 and .4 below 1, the right sides exceeding 1 imply negative exponents. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All four independent equations are covered in paired complete claims. The stem explicitly separates each k and retains the 1.003 near-one value."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "698",
      "question_ids": [
        "q698-increasing",
        "q698-decreasing"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "3e599914ec4aeb5a91cdb4f409a27fa284cf586cadd4303f74af4f0185c0143c",
      "author_solution": "698. Task: graphically solve two intersection problems.\na roots 0 and 1, since 2^0=1 and 2^1=2. f=2^x-x-1 is strictly convex\nand these two roots exhaust its zeros.\nb unique root 0: (.3)^x is strictly decreasing and x+1 increasing;\nthe LHS positive also excludes x<=-1. Actual graphs must show both curves.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 698"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q698-increasing/B: The visible crossings at x=0 and x=1 satisfy the equation; the frozen convexity argument excludes further roots. q698-decreasing/C: At x=0 both sides are 1; the exponential decreases and the line increases, so this is the unique crossing. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (2 questions). The original graph-solving equation appears unchanged with its actual analytic plot. All crossings rather than one observed root are requested; free construction is lost, graphical crossing interpretation retained. The second source equation is separate from its increasing counterpart and uses graph B. Decreasing/increasing geometry and root-set completeness are retained."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "699",
      "question_ids": [
        "q699-near-miss"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "84baf18dc7ab4164ef9d4ccda1ff61e5f3782954ab42b9930d4ac01c7d71cb4c",
      "author_solution": "699. Task: graphically solve 2^x=x^2-2, without assuming two roots.\nFor x<0, f=2^x-x^2+2 has f'>0; f(-2)<0<f(-1), giving one negative\nroot, numerically -1.5316287576583387 by independently computed bisection.\nFor x>=0, f'=ln(2)2^x-2x, f''=(ln(2))^2 2^x-2 has exactly one zero.\nThus f' first decreases then increases, with one zero between 0 and 1\n(local maximum) and one between 3.2 and 3.3 (local minimum).\nAt the latter, f>2^3.2-3.3^2+2>.29, and f(0)=3; hence no nonnegative roots.\nThe minimum occurs at x about 3.2124325245 and is positive. A graph should\nshow the near-miss on the positive side rather than crop it away.\nGraph readout can locate a root in (-1.6,-1.5), not prove completeness on R.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 699"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q699-near-miss/B: The frozen root lies between -1.6 and -1.5; the fine negative-side grid supports that bracket. The reused complete source proof excludes positive roots. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). The original nonlinear crossing task retains the negative root, approximate precision and positive near-miss, with two clearly labeled finite windows. The source-frozen global completeness proof is reused; no finite plot is called a global certificate. Free construction is lost."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "700",
      "question_ids": [
        "q700-linear-exponents",
        "q700-quadratic"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "143100737e3ab7b453988bb20743a31578acad3c5ec4075a1be485bd3f10c4f9",
      "author_solution": "700. Task: six root sets.\na 3^x=3^-2/3 gives {-2/3}.\nb .4^x=(2/5)^x=25/4=(2/5)^-2 gives {-2}.\nc 7^x=7^-1/5 gives {-1/5}.\nd (.8*1.25)^10=1 gives {0}.\ne .2^x=5^-x=5^3 gives {-3}.\nf 6^(x^2-1)=1 implies x^2-1=0, roots {-1,1}.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 700"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q700-linear-exponents/A: The right sides are 3^(-2/3) and 7^(-1/5); injectivity gives the stated roots. q700-linear-exponents/B: 6 1/4=(2/5)^-2 and 125=(1/5)^-3. q700-linear-exponents/C: (4/5)*1.25=1, so the right side is 1 and the nonunit base gives x=0. q700-quadratic/C: The exponent must be zero: x^2-1=0 gives both -1 and 1. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (2 questions). All five linear-exponent source equations retain radicands, mixed number and decimal bases. The sixth quadratic exponent is separately reviewed; paired recognition does not omit an equation. The source quadratic exponent remains a complete root-set task, including both signs and contrast with an all-real claim."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "701",
      "question_ids": [
        "q701-sets"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "d0e4645529120a536a1786c6f3c9ca57bd558d6b7af3d0e39378b40386abd7a9",
      "author_solution": "701. Task: six inequalities.\na x>3/2; b x<2/3; c x>-1/2;\nd .7^x>100/49=.7^-2 implies x<-2;\ne .2^x<25=.2^-2 implies x>-2;\nf (1/3)^x>(1/3)^(2/3) implies x<2/3.\nAll bounds are strict; bases below 1 reverse order.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 701"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q701-sets/A: Increasing bases 2 and 5 give thresholds 3/2 and 2/3 with displayed directions. q701-sets/B: 10^(-1/2) is the reciprocal square root; .7^-2=100/49 is the mixed number and the decreasing base reverses direction. q701-sets/C: .2^-2=25 and (1/3)^(2/3)=cuberoot(1/9); both decreasing comparisons give the displayed directions. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All six original strict inequalities remain in complete paired solution claims. Irrational thresholds, mixed-number reciprocal and decreasing-base reversals are covered."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "702",
      "question_ids": [
        "q702-linear"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "0bc635005ed94ce5f19e7b51dce9bd81d5c2882e2a63e13410cc9372a693564f",
      "author_solution": "702. Task: four exponent-linear equations.\na 5x=-7 => -7/5.\nb (-2x)=-3 for base .5 => x=3/2 (equivalently 2^(2x)=2^-3?\nCorrection of the alternate conversion: .5^(-2x)=2^(2x), .125=2^-3,\nso 2x=-3 and x=-3/2. The direct base-.5 exponent of .125 is +3,\nso -2x=3. Final independent result b=-3/2.)\nc 3x=0 =>0. d 7x=2 =>2/7.\nThe retained correction records the original independent arithmetic fault;\nthe final result is checked by substitution, not by an answer file.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 702"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The initially wrong sign is explicitly retained as a historical fault and then corrected in the same field; the final result, not the obsolete intermediate claim, agrees with the frozen independent solution. q702-linear/B: Exponent equations give -7/5,-3/2,0,2/7, respectively. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All four source exponent equations remain in order with zero exponent and two decreasing-base transformations. Written algebra is replaced by full-vector recognition."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "703",
      "question_ids": [
        "q703-radicals",
        "q703-compatibility",
        "q703-products"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "40e05e5fdf8b9efd32e5834d2fb14b57461fd3dde2e4dab84d84d02fb97c4643",
      "author_solution": "703. Task: ten exponential equations.\na 2^(x/2)=2^10 =>20.\nb 10^(x/2)*10^-1=10^3 =>8.\nc 2^(6x-2)=2^(6x-15) would require -2=-15; no roots.\nd 2^(4x-2)=2^(70-5x) =>x=8.\ne 10^x=10^-3 =>-3.\nf 6^x=6^3 =>3.\ng 10^(x-1)=10^-3 =>-2.\nh 28^(1-x)=28^-1 =>2.\ni (sqrt(12)*sqrt(3))^x=6^x=6^-1 =>-1.\nj (sqrt(14))^(x-1)=1 =>1.\nThe impossible c is an essential distinction, not a missing numeric blank.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 703"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q703-radicals/B: x/2=10 and x/2-1=3 give 20 and 8. q703-compatibility/A: The first equation reduces to 6x-2=6x-15, a contradiction. q703-compatibility/C: 4x-2=70-5x gives 9x=72 and x=8. q703-products/A: Product bases 10 and 6 give exponents -3 and 3. q703-products/B: 10^(x-1)=10^-3 gives -2; 28^(1-x)=28^-1 gives 2. q703-products/C: sqrt(12)*sqrt(3)=6 gives x=-1; sqrt(7)*sqrt(2)=sqrt(14) is nonunit and its exponent must be zero, giving 1. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (3 questions). The two source radical equations retain the extra factor .1. They are not simplified in the prompt; the other eight source parts are reviewed separately. Source c/d remain independent equations. Empty set versus all-real set and linear-coefficient division are explicit; cancellation is not mistaken for identity. All remaining six source product equations retain shared shifts, product bases and radicals. Complete paired root claims cover every part."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "704",
      "question_ids": [
        "q704-product-bounds"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "cd53b20211c9ca87982c4215c17b35500b3c08c0498498541c428414e865fdf6",
      "author_solution": "704. Task: three inequalities.\na 3^(x/2+1)>3^-3 => x>-8.\nb 3^(3x+1-x)=3^(2x+1)<3^-1 =>x<-1.\nc 2^(-6x+1/3)>1 =>x<1/18.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 704"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q704-product-bounds/B: The three exponent comparisons give x>-8, x<-1, x<1/18. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All three original inequalities retain product factors and strictness. Increasing-base conversion still requires solving the linear exponent direction, including the negative coefficient."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "705",
      "question_ids": [
        "q705-roots"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "3821bd14be148184f1b7f86cbb5c85538f074e4e8091a15549f47897702e2dc8",
      "author_solution": "705. Task: three equations.\na 3^(4x-4)=3^5 =>9/4.\nb 6^(4x+6)=6^3 =>-3/4.\nc 8^-1*2^3=1 =>(x-1)^2=0 =>{1}, one distinct root.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 705"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q705-roots/A: 4(x-1)=5 gives x=9/4. q705-roots/B: 4x+6=3 gives x=-3/4. q705-roots/C: The right side is 1 and (x-1)^2=0 gives only x=1. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All three equations retain shifted powers and the repeated quadratic root; singleton/extra-root alternatives judge completeness."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "706",
      "question_ids": [
        "q706-factor"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "c042572f859fa53aa126a34ee2013e3e7efed7159c7486df719510c96f8dcc4b",
      "author_solution": "706. Task: four factorizable sums.\na (1+4)2^x=20 =>2^x=4 =>2.\nb (1+3)3^x=12 =>3^x=3 =>1.\nc (5+1)5^(x-1)=30 =>5^(x-1)=5 =>2.\nd (1+4+16)4^(x-1)=84 =>4^(x-1)=4 =>2.\nAddition of exponentials is factored, never handled as addition of exponents.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 706"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q706-factor/C: Common factors give 5*2^x=20,4*3^x=12,6*5^(x-1)=30,21*4^(x-1)=84, hence 2,1,2,2. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All four original sums are preserved in a full-vector task. Common-factor algebra is not supplied in the stem; free written derivation is the response loss."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "707",
      "question_ids": [
        "q707-forward-domain",
        "q707-reverse-domain"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "f07c97eebb83a61d35fe07d035edb31335e854f41c298815c81b33e87a69bb76",
      "author_solution": "707. Task: prove both directions of a geometric/exponential correspondence.\nThe approved modern erratum ca -> ca^x is already in the seed. It does not\nsettle domains. For indexing n=1,2,... and r!=0, u_n=u_1 r^(n-1)\n=c a^n with a=r,c=u_1/r, including negative r on this integer domain.\nConversely u_n=c a^n satisfies u_(n+1)=a u_n for integer n>=1.\nFor a!=0 and c=0 this is the zero sequence, valid under the recurrence\ndefinition but adjacent quotients are undefined. For a=0 and n>=1 all\nterms are zero; a source geometric sequence (u_1!=0,0,0,...) cannot be\nrepresented by c*0^n with finite c. Thus an unrestricted 'any' in a is\nfalse under that recurrence definition and starting index.\nFor n=0,1,..., u_n=u_0 r^n, but r=0 needs an explicit 0^0 convention\nor a separate n=0 definition. Negative a is legitimate on integers but\nnot as an everywhere-defined real-exponent function; a>0,a!=1 belongs\nto the usual real exponential convention, not the unrestricted sequence claim.\nN's inclusion of zero and geometric-sequence nonzero-term requirements are\nabsent from seed. A substantive conditional validity/counterexample question\ncan be keyed only with its integer domain and recurrence definition visible.\nBoth unrestricted written proofs and resolution of the source convention\nremain unavailable; do not silently prove a restricted theorem as the original.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 707"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "Both mapping directions retain the integer-index/recurrence restrictions, zero-sequence exceptions and unresolved original conventions; this is not approval of the unrestricted original proof claim. q707-forward-domain/A: Independently substituting c=u1/r and a=r gives u_n=u1*r^(n-1) for r!=0; integer powers admit negative r. q707-forward-domain/B: This recurrence has r=0. If ca=1 then c and a are nonzero, forcing ca^2=a!=0, contradiction with the second term. q707-reverse-domain/A: For a!=0, integer powers exist and ca^(n+1)=a*ca^n; if c=0 both sides remain zero. q707-reverse-domain/C: a=0 gives 0^n=0 for n>=1; adding n=0 requires a separate 0^0 convention. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (2 questions). Source 707a uses the registered missing-exponent correction. The view explicitly chooses positive integer indexing and a broader recurrence definition, retaining the ordinary nonzero-ratio representation theorem and adding the zero-ratio obstruction. This is a disclosed convention qualification, not a hidden restriction of negative ratios. Proof production/induction is lost; verifying the coefficient construction is retained. Source 707b converse is preserved under the explicitly stated recurrence convention. Zero coefficients, zero base, natural-index convention and invalid extension to all-real x are separately judged. Ordinary nonzero-ratio convention remains in the frozen source rather than being silently rewritten."
    },
