    },
    {
      "source_number": "712",
      "question_ids": [
        "q712-nesting"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "009fc6c18ce6a4eca978e90d45c0fff2cc2d15673c4de23316b7c09d43b2f84f",
      "author_solution": "712. Task: four nested operations.\n{[17.3]}={17}=0; [{-25.7}]=[.3]=0;\n{[15]}={15}=0; [{23}]=[0]=0.\nGeneral facts: fractional part of any integer is zero, floor of a remainder\nin [0,1) is zero. These are all-real statements, not merely four memorized zeros.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 712"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q712-nesting/A: The inner floors in expressions 1/3 are integers; inner fractions in 2/4 lie in [0,1). Each outer operation therefore returns zero. q712-nesting/B: floor(t) is an integer, so its fractional part is zero for every real t. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All four original expressions occur in the stem and are jointly judged by A; a general nested identity is added. Separate nested calculation production is lost, but order of operations and floor/fraction range remain."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "713",
      "question_ids": [
        "q713-small-witness",
        "q713-large-witness",
        "q713-carry-witness"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "38a66d0b84cad427c9cc87080afc47fb9038b3df3257db2149ba00fd0f8586ff",
      "author_solution": "713. Task: generate several examples for each of three inequalities.\na union over n in Z of [n,n+.15); e.g -1,-.9,0,.1,1.1.\nb union (n+2/3,n+1); e.g -.2,.7,1.8 (2/3 itself excluded).\nc let f={a}. For f<.95, {a+.05}=f+.05, so strict inequality fails.\nFor .95<=f<1, {a+.05}=f-.95, so the LHS exceeds RHS by 1.\nHence union [n+.95,n+1), e.g -.05,.96,1.99. Boundary .95 included.\nSelecting valid witnesses/characterizations is not producing new examples.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 713"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q713-small-witness/A: frac(-1)=0<.15. q713-small-witness/C: frac(.1)=.1<.15. q713-small-witness/D: floor(-.9)=-1 and frac(-.9)=.1<.15. q713-large-witness/A: .7>2/3. q713-large-witness/B: frac(-.2)=.8>2/3. q713-large-witness/E: frac(1.8)=.8>2/3. q713-carry-witness/B: .95+.05=1; the left is 1 and the new fractional part is zero. q713-carry-witness/C: frac(-.05)=.95 and frac(-.05+.05)=0; the carry inequality holds. q713-carry-witness/E: frac(1.99)=.99; adding .05 crosses an integer and changes the right remainder to .04. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (3 questions). Source 713a request for several witnesses is converted to selecting all valid candidates, including a negative value, an integer and the strict boundary. Unlimited example generation is lost, validity judgment retained. Source 713b retains strict >2/3, positive/negative witnesses and an exact boundary. Generated witnesses become candidate evaluation. Source 713c carry inequality is unchanged. Candidates discriminate the included carry threshold, integer case and negative witness. No carry rule is printed in the prompt."
    },
    {
