      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q715-floor-systems/A: 0<=.21+x<1 gives [-.21,.79). q715-floor-systems/B: 2<=y-.36<3 gives [2.36,3.36). q715-floor-systems/D: -9<=1.7+z<-8 gives [-10.7,-9.7). q715-fraction-lattices/A: frac(.58+x)=.58 iff x is an integer. q715-fraction-lattices/B: y-.39=n+.61 gives y=n+1; as n ranges over Z this is exactly Z. q715-fraction-lattices/C: 1.62+x=n+.65 gives x=n-.97, equivalently Z+.03. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (2 questions). All three source floor equations retain variables, signs and decimal offsets. Half-open complete intervals are required, including the floor of a negative number. All three source fractional equations retain all-integer shifts rather than singleton answers. The n domain is explicit and no positivity restriction is imposed."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "716",
      "question_ids": [
        "q716-fraction-counterexample",
        "q716-floor-counterexample"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "61d9adaa3cc38188233c60916947f8292b28498f09f8705584f2fd2991ca53ef",
      "author_solution": "716. Task: produce a counterexample to each proposed equality.\na {5.41+x}={5.41}+{x} fails exactly when {x}>=.59;\ne.g x=.6 gives .01 !=1.01. Integer shifts do not repair a carry.\nb [2x]=2[x]+floor(2{x}); fails exactly when {x}>=.5;\ne.g x=.5 gives 1 !=0. Equal endpoint .5 is important.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 716"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q716-fraction-counterexample/A: frac(6.01)=.01 whereas .41+frac(.6)=1.01. q716-fraction-counterexample/C: frac(5.01)=.01 whereas .41+frac(-.4)=1.01. q716-fraction-counterexample/D: 5.41+.59=6; left remainder zero and right sum 1, so the boundary is a counterexample. q716-floor-counterexample/A: floor(1)=1 versus 2*floor(.5)=0. q716-floor-counterexample/B: floor(-.5)=-1 versus 2*floor(-.25)=-2. q716-floor-counterexample/E: floor(3.5)=3 versus 2*floor(1.75)=2. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (2 questions). Source 716a existence of any counterexample becomes all-candidate checking. The carry threshold, equality cases and negative remainder are retained without a supplied counterexample solution. Source 716b retains doubled argument versus doubled floor. Integer, sub-half, positive-half and negative cases require equality testing. Free witness generation is lost."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "717",
      "question_ids": [
        "q717-product-counterexamples"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "dde0c47e860908e008bb61c592d1c3abe0fb6cd52d5558128d34d7dbfd676444",
      "author_solution": "717. Task: decide product identity and justify.\nIt is not universal: a=b=1.5 gives {ab}=.25 but {a}{b}=.25,\nso that pair is NOT a counterexample. Use a=1.2,b=2.3:\n{ab}=.76, {a}{b}=.06; cross terms involving integer parts matter.\nAlso a=b=.8 yields .64=.64, a valid coincidence. A proper validity question\nmust distinguish genuine counterexamples from equality examples.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 717"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q717-product-counterexamples/A: Product 2.76 has remainder .76 while .2*.3=.06, unequal. q717-product-counterexamples/D: Product 1 has remainder zero while .5*.4=.2, unequal. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). The source universal product question becomes explicit counterexample judgment, including genuine equality examples to prevent assuming every noninteger pair refutes the identity."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "718",
      "question_ids": [
        "q718-sum-counterexamples"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "1bee69c10510b3d21f1a79f5b04ea94759b4a4b0a80287690c19aa60c0977160",
      "author_solution": "718. Task: decide additivity of floor and justify.\nLet a=m+r,b=n+s, r,s in [0,1). floor(a+b)=m+n+floor(r+s).\nEquality holds iff r+s<1. Example .6,.6 violates it (1 !=0);\nnegative examples are allowed, e.g -.2,-.3 give -1 !=-2.\nThe failure is a carry, not a universal defect for negative numbers.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
