    },
    {
      "source_number": "721",
      "question_ids": [
        "q721-tiled-set"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "c49ec7348e51bf24a9d5c58071dd3f02f702b6fb0a978391bc375c32e5b45b2c",
      "author_solution": "721. Task: plot floor(x)=floor(y).\nUnion over all n in Z of [n,n+1) x [n,n+1). Unit diagonal squares,\nnot just the line y=x and not the whole band |x-y|<1.\nLeft and bottom edges included; top and right excluded within each tile.\nAt every integer diagonal corner (n,n) the next tile includes that point.\nExample (.2,.8) qualifies, (.9,1.1) does not despite separation <1.\nA finite visible window must state the pattern repeats for all integer n.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 721"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q721-tiled-set/A: Equal floors mean both coordinates are in the same [n,n+1), giving exactly the displayed infinite union. q721-tiled-set/C: Both .2,.8 have floor zero; every integer diagonal pair has equal floors and belongs to the next tile. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). The source graph of equal floors becomes interpretation of the actual repeating half-open tiles. Infinite integer union, interior membership and mixed boundaries are retained. No finite five-tile truncation is imposed."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "722",
      "question_ids": [
        "q722-exponent-laws"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "0b0f1c5647a4da621cd5fc2573cd373b4d51175482b3596ca95aae209d1bc9b1",
      "author_solution": "722. Task: four expressions as base-10 powers.\na 10^(a+b); b 10^(3a); c 10^(b/2); d 10^(2a/3+b/5).\nThe original defines positive bases 31.5 and .42 so all real roots used\nhere are well-defined. Multiplication adds exponent contributions.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 722"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The transformation, final responses and endpoint/domain distinctions match the already frozen independent solution. No new source solve was performed. q722-exponent-laws/A: Multiplication of powers with base 10 adds a+b. q722-exponent-laws/B: Cubing multiplies a by 3. q722-exponent-laws/D: The positive square root of 10^b is 10^(b/2). q722-exponent-laws/E: The two exponents are 2a/3 and b/5, added under multiplication. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). All four original expressions retain a,b and root orders. Products, ordinary powers and fractional powers are distinguished with no intermediate exponent already supplied in the stem."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "723",
      "question_ids": [
        "q723-positive-powers",
        "q723-small-and-product"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "e96feab5cdb511ac5cb7cb83b92988231a2f10ccc33ac2e148fe53546ef96a16",
      "author_solution": "723. Task: use the actual graph y=10^x to evaluate four expressions.\nLet L(z)=the x-coordinate of the height z on that graph.\na 10^(L(1.95)/5) approx1.1428965514.\nb 10^(5L(23)/6) approx13.6387069101.\nc 10^(2L(.0041)/3) approx .0256166640.\nd 10^(.2L(.18)+L(400)/3) approx5.2288698425.\nIndependently computed log inputs:\n.2900346114,1.3617278360,-2.3872161433,-.7447274949,2.6020599913.\nA normalized visible graph t in [0,1], y=10^t in [1,10] supports\nall lookups by scientific notation and integer shifts; it is an authored\ngraph, not a recovered source image. Graph reading is approximate; don't\nclaim exact displayed decimals or evidence of the learner drawing a graph.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 723"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "Computed internal numerical values are not recast as historical-image precision; authored graph/table normalization and recognition losses remain those already frozen. q723-positive-powers/B: First compatible; second compatible. Both coordinates are required for a true complete pair. q723-small-and-product/A: First compatible; second compatible. Both coordinates are required for a true complete pair. Free graph construction and independent written workings remain the previously disclosed choice-response production losses. RECOVERY1 removes the essential quantitative graph-bypass defect without claiming to restore handwritten production or historical-image identity. The shared approved y=10^x mantissa graph remains unannotated and its normalization is reusable domain knowledge, not an answer to either repaired question. Source quantities are disjoint; no coordinate estimate or true-pair letter from one gives the other. Both now independently require two quantitative estimates, and neither supplies a solved logarithm for the other."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "724",
      "question_ids": [
        "q724-lookup-table"
      ],
      "author_solution_sha256": "068f2e76d409c2870b33782d6913b6b1849f98a8c2e9cbfaff60b635fc1d235c",
      "author_solution": "724. Task: use a numerical table of 10^x to express four numbers as powers.\nIndependently computed exponents:\na .2024883171; b -.4282911682; c 1.7138264244; d 2.3012470886.\nNormalize .373=3.73*10^-1, 51.74=5.174*10^1, 200.1=2.001*10^2.\nAn authored table bracketing normalized mantissas gives exponent intervals:\na (.20,.21); b (-.43,-.42); c (1.71,1.72); d (2.30,2.31).\nThe source numerical table is not supplied in this empty-prose lesson.\nAny new table must be visible and attributed as authored lookup data.\nRecognition/bracketing loses independently producing a decimal approximation\nto a specified number of places; source specifies no precision.",
      "independent_authority_reused": {
        "path": ".tmp/ld-s10y-lesson/resume182-7-C5Ex/math-independent-FIRST/source-solutions-FIRST.record0444",
        "sha256": "92ebd86c58ebab876c51b7a2ffe63d69c9f779232628483a4356c4e248a47db4",
        "section": "### 724"
      },
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "Computed internal numerical values are not recast as historical-image precision; authored graph/table normalization and recognition losses remain those already frozen. q724-lookup-table/C: Nearest mantissa columns are .20,.57,.71,.30; adding the decade characteristics 0,-1,1,2 gives .20,-.43,1.71,2.30. Reviewed every permitted view and all its options for this source (1 question). The original numerical-table inverse task is retained with an explicitly authored eight-entry replacement, not claimed historical source/table identity. Normalization, nearest mantissa and approximate equality are explicit. Precision is reduced to .01 exponents versus the frozen reference-table .0001 readings; original-table use/interpolation and free lookup production are lost, but table inversion and decade shifts remain. Values require comparison of distances, not copying an exact target cell."
    }
